  "article": {
    "images": [],
    "vocabulary": [],
    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Have you ever met a Brit in person? If so, I "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "bet",
            "zh": "敢说"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " that person said sorry to you at least once!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Sorry” is probably the most commonly heard "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b1",
            "number": 1
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " here in the UK. Most Brits make sure they say sorry when they "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "sneeze",
            "zh": "打喷嚏"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", correct someone else’s mistake, or are "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b2",
            "number": 2
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " for a meeting. But they also “apologise” for the weather. And they often say sorry to you when you "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "bump",
            "zh": "碰撞"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " into them on the street. They say sorry when they can’t hear what you’re saying. They also say sorry before sitting down next to you."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "So just how many times a day does the average Brit say sorry? According to a 2016 survey of more than 1,000 Brits, the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b3",
            "number": 3
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " was eight. And one in eight Brits says sorry up to 20 times a day!"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "“Sorry” comes from the Old English word “sarig”. At first, it meant “"
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b4",
            "number": 4
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", very upset, and full of sorrow”. However, we aren’t always in pain or upset when we say sorry. The British may say sorry far more often than people in other "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b5",
            "number": 5
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", but this doesn’t mean we feel sorry more often. "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b6",
            "number": 6
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ", we say sorry as a way of showing good manners. It’s just a more "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b7",
            "number": 7
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " way of saying, “We don’t really care, but we feel we should say something.”"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Some researchers think the "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b8",
            "number": 8
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " of saying sorry is linked to the British "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "class system",
            "zh": "阶级制度"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". We apologise because we think we may have more "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b9",
            "number": 9
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " than those we are talking to."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "If you visit a British family, your host may say sorry to you several times a day. Don’t feel strange or embarrassed. We are really sorry for saying sorry "
          },
          {
            "t": "blank",
            "id": "b10",
            "number": 10
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". Wait, did I just say “sorry” twice in one sentence? Sorry about that!"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
